00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:21.020
Let's get into it. Here's something that's going to change how you turn research into something you

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:26.880
can actually watch and share. Google just added a new feature to its notebook LM tool.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:32.860
This tool now turns whatever documents or notes you upload into short vertical videos that

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:37.900
look like TikTok clips. Each one runs about 60 seconds and includes narration,

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:43.740
visuals, and texts that pops on screen. Think of it like handing a messy stack of homework

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:49.100
papers to a super organized friend who instantly edits them into a fast-paced mini-movie.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:55.020
The system pulls out the key moments from your sources and arranges them so the story flows quickly.

00:00:55.820 --> 00:01:01.280
Google shared an example where it took a long article about Australia's war efforts and turned

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:07.200
it into something watchable in under a minute. This matters because students and creators often

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:13.520
spend hours building slides or editing clips for projects and social posts. Now the work happens

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:19.160
almost right away. It connects directly to things like class presentations, quick YouTube ideas,

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:24.880
or short recaps of books you are reading for school. For you personally, it means your phone

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:30.640
or laptop can help turn boring research into something visual and fun without needing any video

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:36.400
editing experience. The feature is rolling out now to people who already pay for Google's AI

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:43.360
ultra or pro plans. To try it yourself, head to the notebook LM website, upload a few sources

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:49.020
like a PDF or web article and look for the new option to generate a vertical AI clip.

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:54.180
Start with something short like the backstory of your favorite game and see what it creates.

00:01:54.980 --> 00:02:00.680
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, The Deep Dive. We are going to look under the hood

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:06.580
at how notebook LM actually builds these clips. You know how when you are scrolling TikTok,

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:11.940
the app already knows which short clips will keep you watching by picking out the most interesting

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:18.060
parts from longer videos. Now picture that same idea but apply it to your own notes instead of

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:23.780
videos. First, the system reads everything you upload and breaks it into small chunks like individual

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:30.320
sentences or facts. Then it decides which chunks are the most important or surprising, almost like

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:36.180
choosing the best highlights for a sports recap show. Next, it matches those highlights with simple

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:41.220
visuals and a voice that reads them out loud while lining everything up so the whole thing flows

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:47.240
in about 60 seconds. The whole process feels like turning a long book report into a movie trailer

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:53.740
that still tells the main story without adding anything extra. That is what notebook LM is doing

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:59.760
when it makes these clips. It is not inventing new information. It is simply smartly picking and

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:04.880
arranging what you already gave it. Once you see the steps laid out like that, the whole thing

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:10.460
starts to feel less like magic and more like a clever sorting system you can actually picture in your

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:17.120
head. Let's move on to some cool stuff you can try right now. First up is an AI keyboard that

